Database migrations (Alembic).

Everyday use, from backend/:

    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "what changed"   # after editing app/db/models.py
    alembic upgrade head                                # apply
    alembic downgrade -1                                # undo the last one
    alembic current                                     # where is this database?
    alembic check                                       # do the models and migrations agree?

Which database? Whatever DATABASE_URL says, defaulting to the local SQLite
file. alembic.ini deliberately holds no URL - env.py reads it from
app/db/session.py so there is exactly one answer to that question. To
migrate somewhere else, set the variable:

    DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://... alembic upgrade head

Three things worth knowing before writing one:

  * ALWAYS read an autogenerated revision before committing it. Alembic
    detects added and removed things well and renames not at all - it sees
    a rename as a drop plus an add, which on a real database means losing
    the column's data.

  * Migrations run in `render_as_batch` mode because SQLite cannot ALTER a
    column, and SQLite is what local development and the entire test suite
    run on. Batch mode rebuilds the table. No effect on Postgres.

  * The app runs `alembic upgrade head` on startup (app/db/init_db.py), so
    a fresh checkout and the test suite need no setup step. Set
    FIRE_AGENT_AUTO_MIGRATE=0 where migrating should be a deploy step
    instead - which is what a multi-worker deployment wants, since N
    workers booting together would otherwise race to apply the same
    revision.

A database created before this project had migrations is adopted
automatically, once: reconciled to the baseline and stamped. See
app/db/init_db.py, which is where that is implemented and explained, and
tests/test_db_migration.py, which proves it.
